From 380bb64153b19abf9b92f784502e2208ab861f3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: baKon <mike.ski83@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 19:10:48 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] libr4d: --tp-wire wht6 at three ranks, the tiered-int8
 wire over the uneven two-shot

--tp 3 --tp-wire wht6 had no kernel: the lossy rows were world_size 2 (rotated 6-bit) and 4
(tiered int8 over the two-shot). The tiered-int8 two-shot is already written over the world size,
and what kept it at four was the even split it shards by -- numel % (32 x ws) -- which a 2048-wide
row (64 groups) meets at three ranks one step in three.

The kernel now splits ng = numel/32 groups as evenly as they divide, the first ng % ws shards one
group longer, and lays every slot out for the longest shard, so writer and reader agree on one
number. At four ranks the entry still requires numel % 128 and the split is the even one it was.
ar_twoshot_3rank_ti8 is the only lossy row at three, so as at four it resolves at every size; a
message under min_bytes, or not whole groups, goes to the exact one-shot.

r4d_selftest holds it to what a lossy wire can be held to: every rank the same bytes, the declined
messages the exact fold bit for bit, and the rest within the int8 wire's error of the fp32 sum
(relative RMS < 1%), over groups % 3 of 0, 1 and 2 with outliers, to the 20971520 ceiling. The
--tp-wire help, the startup LOSSY warning, README, GUIDE and TP3.md say three ranks.
